 ### [Reconstruction and Compositing Techniques](4646-reconstruction-and-compositing-techniques.md)
-[Reconstruction and Compositing Techniques](4646-reconstruction-and-compositing-techniques.md) encompasses methods that bridge the gap between discrete volumetric data and the continuous optical model required for ray casting, specifically focusing on how input data is reconstructed into a continuous field and how that field is composited along rays. It addresses the problem of aliasing and interpolation errors during sampling, as well as advanced compositing strategies that incorporate reconstruction quality or specific physical approximations. It differs from standard compositing by explicitly modeling the reconstruction kernel's effect on the signal or by modifying the compositing equation with validity weights, rather than treating samples as point values.
+[Reconstruction and Compositing Techniques](4646-reconstruction-and-compositing-techniques.md) constitute the core mathematical and algorithmic framework for Direct Volume Ray Casting, solving the problem of projecting translucent 3D scalar/vector fields onto a 2D image plane by integrating optical properties along viewing rays. This family is distinguished from sibling methods like [Pre-Integration Lookup](2854-pre-integration-lookup.md) or [Binary Swap Compositing](3264-binary-swap-compositing.md) by its fundamental reliance on iterative sample accumulation (compositing) and signal reconstruction (interpolation) to approximate the volume rendering integral, rather than pre-computing lookup tables or using discrete binary logic for blending.
 
 ### [Lighting-Specific Compositing](5940-lighting-specific-compositing.md)
-[Lighting-Specific Compositing](5940-lighting-specific-compositing.md) integrates lighting calculations (e.g., in-scattering, shadows) directly into the volume compositing pipeline rather than treating them as post-processes [G]. It solves the problem of efficiently approximating complex light transport effects within the ray-casting accumulation loop [G]. It differs from general [Compositing](2436-compositing.md) by embedding light-specific state into the accumulation logic, and from [Pre-Integration Lookup](2854-pre-integration-lookup.md) by avoiding precomputed lookup tables in favor of dynamic accumulation [G].
+[Lighting-Specific Compositing](5940-lighting-specific-compositing.md) refers to a family of compositing strategies that integrate lighting effects (such as in-scattering, multi-channel illumination, or boundary-aware shading) directly into the ray-casting accumulation loop, rather than treating lighting as a post-process or separate pass. It solves the problem of correctly integrating volumetric light transport and material interfaces within the depth-aware accumulation state. It sets itself apart from standard front-to-back compositing by modifying the sample contribution $g_k$ or opacity $\alpha_i$ based on lighting models or geometric cues before blending, and differs from [Final Scene Composition](2722-final-scene-composition.md) by handling lighting within the volume ray-caster rather than compositing final rendered layers.
 
