+### Light Value Blending
+Blends four light values (a, b, c, d) into a single packed light value, replacing any zero values with the fourth value before averaging [S8, unofficial]. If a == 0, set a = d; if b == 0, set b = d; if c == 0, set c = d; return `((a + b + c + d) >> 2) & 0xFF00FF` [S8, unofficial].
+
+### Fabric Compositing
+A compositing pass that creates an initial fabric-like texture by combining advected noise fields, silhouette data, and lighting [S9, book]. Pixels not part of the geometry are discarded using the depth buffer [S9, book]. The color channels after $k$ iterations are defined as:
+$$
+R(x,y) = \frac{r \cdot f_{\lambda_2}(x,y)}{8 \cdot f_{\lambda_1}^2(x,y)} + e_{x,y} + \text{light}(\mathcal{L}_{x,y})
+$$
+$$
+G(x,y) = \frac{(1-r) \cdot f_{\lambda_1}(x,y)}{8 \cdot f_{\lambda_2}^2(x,y)} + e_{x,y} + \text{light}(\mathcal{L}_{x,y})
+$$
+$$
+B(x,y) = e_{x,y} + \text{light}(\mathcal{L}_{x,y})
+$$
+Here $f_{\lambda_1}$ and $f_{\lambda_2}$ are the advected fields $p_{\lambda_1}^k$ and $p_{\lambda_2}^k$. $e_{x,y}$ is the silhouette image. $r$ blends between tensor directions. Squaring the denominator emphasizes differences [S9, book]. The blend factor $r$ is a scalar factor used to blend between the two chosen tensor directions [S9, book]. A separate lighting function `light` manipulates intensity without affecting the base color of the mesh structure, with Blinn-Phong shading providing required depth cues [S9, book].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of compositing in volume rendering originated with the foundational work on direct volume ray casting. The "Accumulate" mode, representing standard front-to-back blending, is the most common scheme [S2, unofficial]. Alternative schemes such as "First Hit" (rendering an isosurface by taking the first sample exceeding a threshold), "Averaging" (producing X-ray-like images), and "Maximum Intensity Projection" (MIP) were introduced to handle specific visualization tasks [S2, unofficial]. MIP is often used to extract vessel structures in magnetic resonance angiograms [S2, unofficial].
+Compositing is the final step in object-order volume rendering, where data is ordered in the correct depth order [S1]. It is often the most expensive step in the object-order approach [S1]. Early implementations included Direct Send, where all processors communicate with each other, assigning image tiles to processors and exchanging data [S1]. Binary Swap was developed to reduce network congestion by dividing communication over $\log_2(N)$ rounds, where $N$ is the number of processors, requiring $N$ to be a power of two [S1]. A modified version, 2-3 Swap, was implemented by Yu et al. to overcome the power-of-two limitation [S1]. Peterka et al. proposed Radix-k, a hybrid method combining direct send and binary swap [S1].
 
-The VolumePro system demonstrated support for front-to-back blending, minimum intensity, and maximum intensity blending modes, establishing these as standard blending modes in real-time ray-casting systems [S1, unofficial]. Hardware-based view-independent cell projection methods further expanded the field by enabling commutative blend functions for unsorted cell projection, allowing compositing in arbitrary order [S3].
-
-Parallel compositing techniques, such as binary swap compositing, were developed to address scalability issues in distributed rendering environments, ensuring all processors participate in every stage of the merge [S6, unofficial]. Hybrid methods like Radix-k combine direct send and binary swap approaches to adapt to different problem settings [S6, unofficial].
+The VolumePro system supported Front-to-back blending, Minimum Intensity, and Maximum Intensity blending modes [S2, unofficial]. Other compositing schemes include Accumulate (standard volume rendering), First Hit (rendering an isosurface by taking the first sample that exceeds a threshold), Averaging (producing X-ray-like images), and Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP) [S3, unofficial]. MIP is often used to extract vessel structures in magnetic resonance angiograms [S3, unofficial].
 
